23}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94bae01-23d6-583f-9e93-b3603fdcb947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608723} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db6cc31-0b27-5f3e-8fe6-b65bebc7d951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c13d8a2b-047c-571d-8093-2428d8409929" :question/math-academy-id "q-125114" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{8})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{8})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{8})+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{8})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{8})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{8})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2} & =\\frac{(-16)}{2}=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002df87b-961d-5d8f-8186-916f1e10a70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}e^{-3x}+\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "002df87b-961d-5d8f-8186-916f1e10a70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}e^{-3x}+\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608428} :answer-field/id #uuid "306df2f6-0d41-5fe7-84bf-69f0243c7983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e84e20f1-1924-5f17-9507-878a96060fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-347844" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}+{e}^{-3x}=6x+2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n$y=3{x}^{2}+2x+\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6x+2-{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (6x+2-{e}^{-3x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}+2x)-(-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})+C \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=3{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)+\\frac{{e}^{-3(0)}}{3}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+0+\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\ny=3{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab7f53a-adef-5e01-814e-d36bdde45361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609330} {:answer/id #uuid "7021b029-73f7-5806-953b-6db483e2b56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{36}+\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609436} {:answer/id #uuid "b460f8ae-2b95-58bf-8d77-09d35b38123a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609547} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ce96e7-743f-5c4a-a3a9-391be7e4563d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609598} {:answer/id #uuid "f92ec009-9035-5faa-b0e7-03889d689e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ce96e7-743f-5c4a-a3a9-391be7e4563d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609598} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4f5319f-13a4-5a00-8c15-b93746bd1ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0be75090-22f5-5cad-b570-f81605580502" :question/math-academy-id "q-12574" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}={x}^{5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}-\\frac{1}{6}\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{36}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35da0d43-7a76-5cd4-ad51-e5f1aeb8ca7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35da0d43-7a76-5cd4-ad51-e5f1aeb8ca7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609316} :answer-field/id #uuid "de094a59-c475-59b4-9b19-f7f008cd6084" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ca71c3c-e761-54c7-a3f1-016d2f3ccc4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335519" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ compute $P(X>3)$ round to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X>3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{4})+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{4})+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =0.984375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.984375 \\\\  & ≈0.0156.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c217fc-5e12-575d-a77e-4770d22f3e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40e^{6}+8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12c217fc-5e12-575d-a77e-4770d22f3e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40e^{6}+8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609264} :answer-field/id #uuid "d65eb213-5713-57c3-a619-d4f1b6bb4230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "240cce6d-12ab-530f-9c94-93433051b3e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350961" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=2\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 2\\sqrt{x}\\,dx=\\frac{4{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{4{x}^{3/2}}{3}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}(\\frac{4{x}^{3/2}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{4({e}^{4})^{3/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4})}{3}-\\frac{4(1)^{3/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{3}]-\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4})}{3}-\\frac{8{x}^{3/2}}{9}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{e}^{6}}{3}-(\\frac{8{e}^{6}}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(48{e}^{6}-8{e}^{6}+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}(5{e}^{6}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e1e8e3-e266-5f44-970e-3721617aa010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609291} {:answer/id #uuid "43b38db0-2549-561e-834a-2d5c69f4b80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609343} {:answer/id #uuid "50ac7bc7-1945-5d30-8901-e322a63a1117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609363} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f1b814-f7b6-5cd1-8966-d427dc8e0e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609576} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f9fd65-356a-59ec-9c98-4640b881885d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b38db0-2549-561e-834a-2d5c69f4b80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609343} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7fc0569-8e14-506a-97ee-0c2b63a070bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2be49696-b015-5246-bfd0-28d18b88059a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75008" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{3}).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3})=-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3})+(-\\frac{2}{3})=-3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\frac{2}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3})+(-\\frac{2}{3})+(-\\frac{2}{3})=-4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\frac{2n}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{2n}{3})=-\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{2}{3})$ is also divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e27d6fa-57b5-5a04-bb8b-0a54b2e3f9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}}{2}(3{e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609304} {:answer/id #uuid "4b781413-227b-55aa-9250-af52f32d8452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{10}-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609355} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d662ae-d519-5efb-a20c-975057f714c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}}{2}(3-{e}^{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609573} {:answer/id #uuid "c74c05b3-3b1c-5d37-ac56-46cd57fe5a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}(3{e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609574} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc5d0e1-d2bb-5d53-bf06-452466acf8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{4}}{3}({e}^{6}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e27d6fa-57b5-5a04-bb8b-0a54b2e3f9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}}{2}(3{e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf0587b-a888-5bbb-9f78-1ead6c6f5988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5374c824-156b-5ca6-8046-d8b6e641e558" :question/math-academy-id "q-49717" :question/problem "${\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=2x & \\,⟹\\,v={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}-{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x({x}^{2}){|}_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}-{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{5}){({e}^{5})}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2}){({e}^{2})}^{2}]-{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{10}-2{e}^{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}} \\\\  & =5{e}^{10}-2{e}^{4}-\\frac{{({e}^{5})}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{({e}^{2})}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{10}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{4}}{2}(3{e}^{6}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340f0800-0c26-5cc3-abca-58a22401371e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609310} {:answer/id #uuid "3a81343c-e8e6-5617-a7d8-6ddd9658a78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609329} {:answer/id #uuid "9b28d037-30c3-5935-b3ee-101e54ae0c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609504} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce4b41-8221-5579-a7bc-e4e17bbffb57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609640} {:answer/id #uuid "fadd327f-0790-5c5a-bd5d-c30496b81c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a81343c-e8e6-5617-a7d8-6ddd9658a78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609329} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa500755-dc79-5985-b47d-68869a72ac26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f8d90ff-59cb-5a0d-933d-bdfb4d3dcf00" :question/math-academy-id "q-124061" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{n+2}{4}).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "The given series is an arithmetic series. Its first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1+2}{4}=\\frac{3}{4},\n$$\n\nIts $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{n+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we get the following formula for the $n$th partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{n} & =\\frac{n}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{2}(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{n+2}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{n(5+n)}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the limit of the partial sums, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n(5+n)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{n}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the limit of the sequence of partial sums is infinite, the series is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5436d1a7-8d3b-59f8-80a6-5d878246e66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609373} {:answer/id #uuid "b306d3b7-bbb4-51a6-b8da-4d2ce46a9b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609546} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e7d6f6-2a20-5138-aefe-342aada3d0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609606} {:answer/id #uuid "d72879fa-59ee-5aec-8f24-d0f465deae42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(1-{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609608} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d4796-e888-5944-a6fb-6cb2089e0eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5436d1a7-8d3b-59f8-80a6-5d878246e66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609373} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bde457d-7956-5580-91ca-213eed8d75c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd2cb8b6-4dd3-5139-9be0-ca32f293a728" :question/math-academy-id "q-49738" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int x\\,dx=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2})]-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69a961f2-3275-5ba2-99c5-d2cffeaecbcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{625}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a961f2-3275-5ba2-99c5-d2cffeaecbcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{625}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609415} :answer-field/id #uuid "791fd252-2327-5efb-aebd-45dd6d36be5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc81c280-7003-5b71-a83c-dc4e24442859" :question/math-academy-id "q-335514" :question/problem "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{5}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X\\ge 5).$\n\n$P(X\\ge 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{0}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{544}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{544}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a31517fe-d8fb-5b23-a457-79d49640dfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-(-2)^N}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31517fe-d8fb-5b23-a457-79d49640dfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-(-2)^N}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609520} :answer-field/id #uuid "db812e98-3f95-5458-8bea-1d9e7f3109f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4c3b4c3-9508-5201-98ef-1f9a57024a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-270559" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of the series $\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+3-⋯\\,?$ Express your answer as a function of $N,$ making sure you use a capital letter $(N,$ not $n).$\n\n${S}_{N}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+3-⋯$ is the sum of a geometric series since the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nFirst, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case ${a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $r=-2.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}(1-(-2)^{N})}{1-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}(1-(-2)^{N})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-(-2)^{N})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ba278e9-9008-551b-adff-df3dde8bfde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2}+1}{9\\ln 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ba278e9-9008-551b-adff-df3dde8bfde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2}+1}{9\\ln 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609422} :answer-field/id #uuid "239b027c-ef72-5b2e-99a4-860ab5606393" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186609703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f847056f-ffcd-5c26-97ff-8ef8216b29ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-351018" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}{log}_{4}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}{log}_{4}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}{log}_{4}⁡(9x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡(9x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}{log}_{4}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}-{\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(9x)\\,(x){|}_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}-{\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}x(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{log}_{4}⁡(9\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{9})\\,(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9})-{log}_{4}⁡(9\\cdot \\frac{1}{9})\\,(\\frac{1}{9})]-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9}{log}_{4}⁡({e}^{2})-\\frac{1}{9}{log}_{4}⁡(1)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}x{|}_{1/9}^{{e}^{2}/9} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f7e6c5-bcb1-5241-96d9-13c9e610ba18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610170} {:answer/id #uuid "2a968a91-4521-5fa6-ba45-577d37f796ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 10 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610172} {:answer/id #uuid "4d445022-2a0d-56ba-a956-c0c0818abae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & -10 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610175} {:answer/id #uuid "b39afe8e-8bdd-5fec-b01f-a6f008253d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610188} {:answer/id #uuid "bb21bc03-8383-5527-ae2e-2ce152135f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & -10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b39afe8e-8bdd-5fec-b01f-a6f008253d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610188} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e10e91-06a6-586f-aab6-8c22bbb6153c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17243001-2ab5-5983-a825-00b76f6856bb" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x+42y-60z &=48 \\\\ 8x-56y+80z &=-64 \\\\ 4x-28y+40z &=-32\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-6 & 42 & -60 & 48 \\\\ 8 & -56 & 80 & -64 \\\\ 4 & -28 & 40 & -32\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nR_1:=(-\\frac{1}{6})R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -7 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 8 & -56 & 80 & -64 \\\\ 4 & -28 & 40 & -32\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+(-8)R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -7 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -28 & 40 & -32\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nR_3:=R_3+(-4)R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -7 & 10 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8e436b-5579-5892-86e8-76003fa95932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610176} {:answer/id #uuid "af5900b7-1e60-5464-8f29-16aeb180680a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610187} {:answer/id #uuid "d512c9b7-80b5-5cd8-8e2c-078ec34d0e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610192} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b824d4-6cea-5a59-9580-d20a91bbec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610194} {:answer/id #uuid "fc51d3af-8016-5d4c-945f-91e7ef84f912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc51d3af-8016-5d4c-945f-91e7ef84f912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610199} :answer-field/id #uuid "faedd2be-f383-5b10-8a3d-a06154db2c8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b8e1ec7-df1c-59c0-a9f1-42c9921a14fb" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-12z &=-44 \\\\ 3x-6y-9z &=-33 \\\\ 4x-8y-12z &=-44\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & -8 & -12 & -44 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -9 & -33 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -12 & -44\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nR_1:=(\\frac{1}{4})R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 3 & -6 & -9 & -33 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -12 & -44\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+(-3)R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & -12 & -44\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nR_3:=R_3+(-4)R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & -3 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259a5c2b-98a7-5fac-b64c-563253aa4bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610169} {:answer/id #uuid "355f8589-770b-5c39-8f8d-a2d3b0938ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610173} {:answer/id #uuid "8335e7fa-5847-53ec-a657-a7a51d2a4d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610182} {:answer/id #uuid "d8864bd4-414c-524f-a318-375eb2a3b27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610193} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a3b200-3cc0-54ce-89f0-8d9388c172f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355f8589-770b-5c39-8f8d-a2d3b0938ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610173} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5592f1-32c0-5239-ab52-de8476a3a6c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2f58ab6-d1b7-50da-b3ff-00d8364d5812" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+4z &=-34 \\\\ -9x+18y-18z &=153 \\\\ -7x+14y-14z &=119\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -4 & 4 & -34 \\\\ -9 & 18 & -18 & 153 \\\\ -7 & 14 & -14 & 119\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nR_1:=(\\frac{1}{2})R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 2 & -17 \\\\ -9 & 18 & -18 & 153 \\\\ -7 & 14 & -14 & 119\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+(9)R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 14 & -14 & 119\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nR_3:=R_3+(7)R_1\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\nM\\sim \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 2 & -17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165ecc04-7fbd-5a77-93f6-d22774668d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610508} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a9eb71-d6f4-5e56-a789-44d48c2f4a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610522} {:answer/id #uuid "c05b88fb-a1f5-578d-b994-6446f57d9da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610523} {:answer/id #uuid "c158e98d-9be1-5924-824f-86a906952603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610524} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d12503-47d7-5f59-a1b1-50b38d30a30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7d12503-47d7-5f59-a1b1-50b38d30a30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610530} :answer-field/id #uuid "35cfa76c-d46c-58d5-abac-80705a446e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d6c902f-2435-5203-aa9f-262524ac5a30" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2 e^{x})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=2 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{x})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (2 e^{x})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=2 e^{x}(\\cos x+\\sin x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left((\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left((\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\cdot (2 e^{(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}})\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ &{}+(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (2 e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0342fedd-8d06-51bf-b9ad-5e63f5f5f982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610505} {:answer/id #uuid "173ff202-950a-5940-aabe-82d481b5d461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610509} {:answer/id #uuid "c354a2c8-b0de-585b-8ee4-0a43e6df96a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610525} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0129b4-44ea-5647-bf41-eda356ea7056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{81 \\pi^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610527} {:answer/id #uuid "e16fa939-f833-50c4-9a94-5a392aabfb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "173ff202-950a-5940-aabe-82d481b5d461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610509} :answer-field/id #uuid "f04cda44-d833-5a4f-9cef-56d38d1b0af1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6996047e-7e31-5564-bb70-e58aabf2bfcd" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{3})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 3 x^{2},\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- x^{3})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 3 x^{2})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=- x^{2} \\left(x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(- (\\frac{3 \\pi}{2})^{3})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left((\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left((\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}) \\right)})\\cdot (- 3 (\\frac{3 \\pi}{2})^{2})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- \\frac{27 \\pi^{3}}{8})\\cdot (0) \\\\ &{}+(-1)\\cdot (- \\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a49124e-b359-593a-a72b-ef618ed162eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(3 - \\pi\\right)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a49124e-b359-593a-a72b-ef618ed162eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(3 - \\pi\\right)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610510} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7d6f26-56ba-523c-91e9-3177f7b66fb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ecb8282-6295-5cd9-a5b5-e1bb578640a9" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(4 x + 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}),$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=4 x + 1$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=4,\\quad v'(x)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(4 x + 1)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=-(4 x + 1)\\sin x+4\\cos x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(4 (\\frac{\\pi}{4}) + 1)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\cos{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(1 + \\pi)\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ &{}+(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(3 - \\pi\\right)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(3 - \\pi\\right)}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a274896-90a4-56a0-b45a-65385719edf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-8 - \\pi\\right)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a274896-90a4-56a0-b45a-65385719edf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-8 - \\pi\\right)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610520} :answer-field/id #uuid "3187eef1-7f80-587b-a0fe-39dc0cb198ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de532b59-dfad-573f-bd25-b6a82e67e141" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, if a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=x\\cdot f(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=1,\\quad v'(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (1)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(x - 3)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(- 3 x - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=\\begin{aligned}&((\\frac{\\pi}{4}))\\cdot (\\sin{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(- 3 \\sin{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)} - \\cos{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\cdot (1)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(\\frac{\\pi}{4})\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2})\\cdot (1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-8 - \\pi\\right)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-8 - \\pi\\right)}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7983ebf-024e-5b14-a762-1b01f86ef865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7983ebf-024e-5b14-a762-1b01f86ef865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610735} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f77178e-a4bf-5d5e-8f45-219f0cb3f4b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b714dc9-9e62-5f39-8b9a-2b63e3dba0a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-352125" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}24{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9{ln}^{2}⁡2}(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡(2x))^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=24{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 24{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=8{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 24{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡(2x))}^{2}(8{x}^{3})-\\int (8{x}^{3})(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡(2x))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 24{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{16{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{9{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{16{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{9{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(8{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-\\frac{16}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{16}{9})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{16D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation, we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}24{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(8{ln}^{2}⁡(2x)-\\frac{16}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{16}{9})]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(e/2)^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(8{ln}^{2}⁡(e)-\\frac{16}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\frac{16}{9})-\\frac{(1/2)^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(8{ln}^{2}⁡(1)-\\frac{16}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{16}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}/8}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(8-\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{16}{9})-\\frac{1/8}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(0-0+\\frac{16}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}}{9{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{9{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9{ln}^{2}⁡2}(\\,5{e}^{3}-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b7da14-8958-5dfd-b773-37fa099de70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610579} {:answer/id #uuid "593ba9b4-2560-54e9-85fa-62438277f414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610642} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc63f3d-02c4-58cb-8512-05275f306687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610706} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ec6d65-6999-5cdf-a2ea-ae77b28ccc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610727} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc5d48d-fe6d-5f99-b4be-51f9a02fe55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b7da14-8958-5dfd-b773-37fa099de70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610579} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd9380e9-e321-51fc-b352-594989ae769b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186610895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0ebcc78-d902-5ae5-8ea0-de2d7b504ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64018" :question/problem "The second term of a geometric sequence is $180$ and its fourth term is $5.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is negative." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{4}=5$ and ${a}_{2}=180.$ Substituting $n=4$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{180} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is negative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212c2f90-5389-5f1c-a8c5-09ef41e01757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611124} {:answer/id #uuid "29a0936d-245a-5572-9396-5049d9b87bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611131} {:answer/id #uuid "41187ba3-ad9e-5b81-b760-1c94a8f7e2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{3/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611165} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3f83ff-d632-5353-94dd-c0f686f28434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{5/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611210} {:answer/id #uuid "74c88755-5a07-57fe-89a4-9010e7f156c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a0936d-245a-5572-9396-5049d9b87bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611131} :answer-field/id #uuid "745f04e3-c32a-589f-97fe-e9cc85295ae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00426341-7e95-51ad-8bcb-90d3bbfb5326" :question/math-academy-id "q-100333" :question/problem "${(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{5}÷{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{5}÷{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{3}={(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{5-3}={(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83862c66-402f-5bb2-b241-48e8a217d67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611276} {:answer/id #uuid "8edf3b47-881a-5c74-b57d-77a259a0b709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611301} {:answer/id #uuid "9a947e69-acd7-511d-89cc-f8847f53e96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611313} {:answer/id #uuid "c131d043-46d0-5550-96b7-087a34593da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611364} {:answer/id #uuid "f2316443-8086-59c9-ba70-c388106a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{36})}^{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a947e69-acd7-511d-89cc-f8847f53e96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611313} :answer-field/id #uuid "779f5c57-31a1-5a7c-b982-214e94db3468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e041e3a-8fe0-5f8d-90b5-af52977860be" :question/math-academy-id "q-68343" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{6}÷{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{6}÷{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{6-3}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194fd8af-b040-5b37-b284-479fc28e14fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "194fd8af-b040-5b37-b284-479fc28e14fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611112} :answer-field/id #uuid "80cdbf21-5d83-5f11-a83b-a6feecf8976c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e66bab1-d394-5399-b025-c983e9b8e368" :question/math-academy-id "q-350057" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}6{sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}6{sec}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u){|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3778d736-aab5-5556-a3e1-042d7337041a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611151} {:answer/id #uuid "37a767a9-a744-55dc-b0a7-959d7397321c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611152} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5e7d32-8286-510a-9d70-ec398b208f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186611177} {:answer/id #uuid "696942b4-b16a-5328-9e57-cfc983c7f5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 175921